{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n75 \n \n \nWeek of July 29, 2019 \nOther investigations on which the crew performed work: \n\u2022 \nACME Flame Design, which studies the production and control of soot to optimize oxygen-\nenriched combustion and the design of robust, soot-free flames, is part of a series of independent \nACME experiments using the space station\u2019s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). \nWhile the crew did work on ACME during this week, it was for the s-Flame experiment.  First-round \noperations for Flame Design ended on July 26.  \n \nWeek of August 5, 2019 \nOther investigations on which the crew performed work: \n\u2022 \nACME Flame Design, which studies the production and control of soot to optimize oxygen-\nenriched combustion and the design of robust, soot-free flames, is part of a series of independent \nACME experiments using the space station\u2019s Combustion Integrated Rack (CIR). \nWhile the crew did work on ACME during this week, it was for the s-Flame experiment.  First-round \noperations for Flame Design ended on July 26.  \n \n2021 \nSecond round (inverse flames expect for the first test day) \nWeek of October 25, 2021 \nOther investigations involving the crew: \n\u2022 \nFlame Design studies the production and control of soot, which can adversely affect combustion \nefficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime. Part of the ACME series of studies, this \ninvestigation could lead to more efficient and cleaner burner designs. \n \nWeek of November 1, 2021 \nOther investigations involving the crew: \n\u2022 \nFlame Design studies the production and control of soot, which can adversely affect combustion \nefficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime. Part of the ACME series of studies, this \ninvestigation could lead to more efficient and cleaner burner designs. \n \nWeek of December 13, 2021 \nBetter burning \nThe crew replaced fuel bottles in support of the on-going Flame Design investigati